          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Are there eating habits that tend to make reflux worse?",
          "answer": "Yes, some eating habits are linked with worse reflux. The general clinical reference on gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) lists **large or late meals** and **alcohol** as contributing factors. It also notes that symptoms are often worse after meals, when bending forward, and when lying down. So the timing and size of meals may matter.\n\nThe search did not return detailed advice on specific foods, such as fatty or spicy foods, coffee, or chocolate. It also did not cover how long to wait between eating and lying down. I can't answer those from the material I have, so a pharmacist or doctor is a good place to ask.\n\nThe reference also says a reflux pattern that doesn't respond to usual steps should be reviewed by a clinician, who may consider tests. If you have severe symptoms, trouble swallowing, unexplained weight loss, vomiting blood, or symptoms that are getting worse, seek medical care promptly.\n\nSources: General clinical reference on GERD (condition-gerd); GERD empiric management guideline (guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
